    "key": "frontend-design",
    "name": "frontend-design",
    "description": "Aesthetic direction for new UI or a reshaped screen. Choose a deliberate palette, type and layout for this subject instead of templated defaults; inside an existing app, work within its design system.",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "instructions": "<!-- Source: github.com/anthropics/skills skills/frontend-design (Apache-2.0, Copyright Anthropic, PBC). Modified by Fhara: condensed to fit Fhara's skill prompt budget; added the existing-app rule. See NOTICE and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. -->\n\n# Frontend design\n\n**Existing app first.** If the product already has tokens, a Tailwind theme or a component library, design inside it (see design-system-detection). Distinctiveness here means hierarchy, spacing and composition, never a new palette.\n\n**Ground it in the subject.** Name the product, its audience and the page's primary job before designing. Distinct choices come from the subject's world, its materials and vocabulary.\n\n**Plan, then check the plan.** Write a compact token plan: 4–6 named colours, one or two typefaces with roles and a type scale, a one-sentence layout concept (an ASCII wireframe helps), and two or three principles. Then ask: would I produce this for any similar brief? Revise those parts and say why.\n\n**Avoid the generated-page tells** unless the brief asks for them:\n- cream background with serif display and terracotta accent; near-black with one acid accent;\n- identical rounded cards with the same soft shadow and gradient washes;\n- ALL-CAPS eyebrow labels, `A · B · C` meta strings, `→` appended to buttons, numbered markers on content that is not a sequence;\n- one word in a headline set in another colour or italic.\n\n**Typography carries personality.** One or two families, clearly distinct. Body lines under ~80 characters.\n\n**Restraint.** Spend boldness in one place; keep the rest quiet. Motion only for one orchestrated moment or in answer to a user action. Quality floor without announcing it: responsive to mobile, visible focus, reduced motion respected, accessible contrast.\n\n**Critique your own work** from a screenshot when one is available, then remove one accessory."
